WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.738
Welcome back to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.688 --> 00:00:06.816
This is Chapter One, Episode 6, Part Two.

3
00:00:07.366 --> 00:00:09.841
Next, crisis and recovery.

4
00:00:09.791 --> 00:00:18.216
After about fifteen years of economic growth and progress, Denmark was in 2008 hit by the international financial crisis.

5
00:00:18.216 --> 00:00:25.366
The crisis started in the USA, but spread quickly to Europe, where it became deeper and longer than in the USA.

6
00:00:25.366 --> 00:00:37.779
That also applied in Denmark, where the gross domestic product, that is, the value of the country's total production, shrank by a total of over five percent in 2008 to 09.

7
00:00:37.779 --> 00:00:42.354
That is the biggest setback for the Danish economy since the Second World War.

8
00:00:42.904 --> 00:00:46.529
The crisis also brought some years of higher unemployment.

9
00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:53.842
When the crisis set in, unemployment was the lowest in thirty years, and there was a large surplus on the state budget.

10
00:00:53.842 --> 00:01:02.229
It was turned into a deficit, but Denmark was still not hit by as big problems with state deficits and debt as most other EU countries.

11
00:01:02.229 --> 00:01:16.041
At the same time, Denmark's foreign debt has turned into a large claim, so that other countries owe Denmark more money than the other way around, and the surplus on the trade balance has set repeated records in the last twenty years.

12
00:01:16.041 --> 00:01:21.756
In 2025, the surplus was about three hundred eighty-two billion kroner.

13
00:01:22.306 --> 00:01:32.081
Since the middle of the 2010s, there has again been higher economic growth, a surplus on public finances and lower unemployment in Denmark.

14
00:01:32.031 --> 00:01:39.494
In 2024, the Danish state's revenue was thus one hundred thirty-three billion kroner higher than expenditure.

15
00:01:39.494 --> 00:01:48.606
And unemployment was three point one percent in April 2026, compared with six point zero percent in April 2013.

16
00:01:49.156 --> 00:01:51.968
Now, the challenge for the welfare state.

17
00:01:51.918 --> 00:01:58.731
Anders Fogh Rasmussen was in 2009 elected the new Secretary General of the defence alliance NATO.

18
00:01:58.731 --> 00:02:11.706
He was succeeded as prime minister by his party colleague Lars Løkke Rasmussen of Venstre, who was prime minister from 2009 to 2011, and again from 2015 to 2019.

19
00:02:11.706 --> 00:02:28.368
Two years later, in 2011, the Social Democrats regained power, and Helle Thorning-Schmidt became Denmark's first female prime minister, for the next four years until 2015, when Lars Løkke Rasmussen again became prime minister.

20
00:02:28.918 --> 00:02:39.093
The challenge of moving more people from transfer income and into the labour market has been the motive for numerous Danish welfare reforms over the last thirty to thirty-five years.

21
00:02:39.043 --> 00:02:44.634
Both Lars Løkke Rasmussen's and Helle Thorning-Schmidt's governments headed many such reforms.

22
00:02:45.184 --> 00:02:54.484
One of the biggest long-term challenges for the welfare state in European countries is that there will be fewer people to pay for the welfare that more people are to enjoy.

23
00:02:54.434 --> 00:02:58.871
This is first and foremost because people on average live longer.

24
00:02:58.871 --> 00:03:05.109
As in other Western countries, life expectancy rises by a couple of months every year.

25
00:03:05.109 --> 00:03:07.859
That development is expected to continue.

26
00:03:07.859 --> 00:03:16.380
At the same time, the birth rate has fallen, while expectations of the quality of elderly care and the health service are rising.

27
00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:25.417
That means there will be markedly more elderly people in relation to the number of fifteen to sixty-four-year-olds, and fewer to work and pay for welfare.

28
00:03:25.367 --> 00:03:30.330
At the same time, working life starts later because of more education.

29
00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:37.614
All in all, there is a lack of labour in many places on the Danish labour market, even though there are still people out of work.

30
00:03:38.164 --> 00:03:45.864
Many different reforms have therefore aimed at increasing the labour supply, to get more people to work, earn money and pay tax.

31
00:03:45.814 --> 00:04:01.824
In this connection, the period in which one can receive support as unemployed from an unemployment insurance fund, the A-kasse, has gradually been shortened since the 1990s and since the last reform in the area in 2010 has been two years.

32
00:04:02.374 --> 00:04:12.311
The so-called Retirement Reform from 2011 was meant to ensure a faster and bigger rise in how old people typically were when they withdraw from the labour market.

33
00:04:12.261 --> 00:04:24.099
Further, reforms were agreed in 2012 and 2013 respectively that should ensure that fewer people go on early retirement pension, and that fewer are on cash benefit.

34
00:04:24.099 --> 00:04:31.011
A number of tax reforms have also had the declared goal of increasing the labour supply by lowering tax on labour income.

35
00:04:31.011 --> 00:04:38.322
Students have, through a series of reforms, been encouraged to start their studies earlier and finish them faster.

36
00:04:38.872 --> 00:04:40.597
Then came the pandemic.

37
00:04:40.547 --> 00:04:50.509
A significant challenge in the years 2020 to 22 was a global pandemic with Covid-19, which was caused by the so-called coronavirus.

38
00:04:50.509 --> 00:04:54.272
The virus was discovered in China in 2019.

39
00:04:54.272 --> 00:04:58.859
In Denmark the first case was registered in February 2020.

40
00:04:58.859 --> 00:05:07.322
The government, the Folketing, the Danish Health Authority and others tried to contain the disease through a series of recommendations and restrictions.

41
00:05:07.322 --> 00:05:17.109
They were to ensure that people, for example, did not meet too many other people, that they kept distance from each other and that they washed their hands in sanitiser.

42
00:05:17.109 --> 00:05:25.404
A main purpose of the restrictions was to prevent too many people being infected at once, so that the hospitals were not overloaded by many admissions.

43
00:05:25.954 --> 00:05:31.866
Therefore, you had to wear face masks on buses, trains and in shops, among other things.

44
00:05:31.816 --> 00:05:36.254
You also could not travel as freely between Denmark and abroad as normal.

45
00:05:36.254 --> 00:05:41.354
Schools were also closed in periods, and pupils were taught at home via the internet.

46
00:05:41.354 --> 00:05:53.718
In addition, many restaurants, cafés, museums and theatres were closed in periods, and football matches and other sports took place without spectators, just as many worked from home.

47
00:05:54.268 --> 00:06:02.093
The health service tested thousands of people daily to find the infected, who then had to stay home for some time so they did not infect more.

48
00:06:02.043 --> 00:06:11.016
The coronavirus has mutated several times into ever more contagious variants, and the disease has so far hit the country in three big waves.

49
00:06:11.566 --> 00:06:16.903
An important means of fighting the disease in the long term was to vaccinate the population.

50
00:06:16.853 --> 00:06:26.266
It took only about one year from the outbreak of the disease to develop vaccines against coronavirus, and in December 2020 the first Dane was vaccinated.

51
00:06:26.266 --> 00:06:33.403
Since then, most, about ninety percent of Denmark's population, have been vaccinated with two or three jabs.

52
00:06:33.403 --> 00:06:41.130
Globally, almost seven million people were registered dead with coronavirus, including almost ten thousand in Denmark.

53
00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:44.330
And finally, the political map today.

54
00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:48.792
In recent years, more new parties have come into Danish politics.

55
00:06:48.792 --> 00:06:58.692
Five of these, Liberal Alliance, the Alternative, the Denmark Democrats, the Moderates and the Citizens' Party, were elected to the current Folketing.

56
00:06:59.242 --> 00:07:04.517
Liberal Alliance was elected to the Folketing for the first time in 2011.

57
00:07:04.467 --> 00:07:13.529
The party arose with a policy of more tax cuts, bigger economic incentive to work, lower public spending and more private market economy.

58
00:07:13.529 --> 00:07:21.542
The Alternative, Alternativet, has, among other things, emphasised that greater focus should be put on the green area.

59
00:07:21.542 --> 00:07:26.979
The party believes that more should be done for the climate, nature and the environment.

60
00:07:26.979 --> 00:07:32.022
The Alternative was elected to the Folketing for the first time in 2015.

61
00:07:32.572 --> 00:07:40.972
In addition, two parties, the Denmark Democrats and the Moderates, emerged shortly before the general election in 2022.

62
00:07:40.922 --> 00:07:49.159
Both were formed by politicians who had earlier been leading members of Venstre, and both were first elected to the Folketing in 2022.

63
00:07:49.159 --> 00:07:55.659
The Denmark Democrats was founded by former Minister for Immigration and Integration Inger Støjberg.

64
00:07:55.659 --> 00:07:58.922
The party is critical of immigration to Denmark.

65
00:07:58.922 --> 00:08:12.034
Furthermore, the Denmark Democrats emphasise better opportunities for the population in the more thinly populated areas of the country, which are found, among other places, in large parts of Jutland.

66
00:08:12.034 --> 00:08:17.747
The Moderates were founded by former prime minister and party leader Lars Løkke Rasmussen.

67
00:08:17.747 --> 00:08:30.259
The party sees itself as a party in the middle of Danish politics and wants the political cooperation to take place "across the middle", with participation of both the Social Democrats and the centre-right parties.

68
00:08:30.259 --> 00:08:35.434
In 2024, the Citizens' Party, Borgernes Parti, emerged.

69
00:08:35.434 --> 00:08:51.336
The name refers to the party's view that power must go back to the citizens, because the state, through, among other things, too many laws and rules, too high taxes and too much digital surveillance, has got too much control over citizens' lives.

70
00:08:51.886 --> 00:09:08.698
After the general election in 2019, the Social Democrat Mette Frederiksen became prime minister in a minority government consisting only of the Social Democrats, with the Social Liberal Party, SF and the Red-Green Alliance as so-called support parties.

71
00:09:08.698 --> 00:09:20.802
And with a new general election in 2022, a majority government was formed with Mette Frederiksen as prime minister, consisting of the Social Democrats, Venstre and the Moderates.

72
00:09:21.352 --> 00:09:25.739
In March 2026, yet another general election was held.

73
00:09:25.689 --> 00:09:32.102
At the election, the trend continued that the old parties collectively get fewer votes than they did before.

74
00:09:32.102 --> 00:09:43.902
Thus the Folketing's five oldest parties, the Social Democrats, SF, Venstre, the Conservatives and the Social Liberal Party, together got fifty-seven percent of the votes.

75
00:09:43.902 --> 00:09:53.427
That is clearly less than eighty-one percent in 2001, seventy-five percent in 2011 and seventy-two percent in 2019.

76
00:09:53.427 --> 00:10:09.314
Almost half of the voters, forty-three percent, thus voted for the seven youngest parties: Liberal Alliance, the Danish People's Party, the Moderates, the Red-Green Alliance, the Denmark Democrats, the Alternative or the Citizens' Party.

77
00:10:09.314 --> 00:10:15.352
After the election, yet another government has been formed with Mette Frederiksen as prime minister.

78
00:10:15.352 --> 00:10:21.228
It consists of the Social Democrats, SF, the Moderates and the Social Liberal Party.

79
00:10:21.778 --> 00:10:51.953
The twelve parties elected to the Folketing in 2026, by share of the vote, are: the Social Democrats, with the letter A, SF with F, Venstre with V, Liberal Alliance with I, the Danish People's Party with O, the Moderates with M, the Conservative People's Party with C, the Red-Green Alliance with Ø, the Social Liberal Party with B, the Denmark Democrats with Æ, the Alternative with Å, and the Citizens' Party with H.

80
00:10:51.953 --> 00:10:57.678
In addition come the four members of the Folketing elected in Greenland and the Faroe Islands.

81
00:10:58.228 --> 00:11:00.815
The chart in the textbook gives the numbers.

82
00:11:00.765 --> 00:11:22.378
In seats: Social Democrats thirty-eight, SF twenty, Venstre eighteen, Liberal Alliance sixteen, Danish People's Party sixteen, Moderates fourteen, Conservatives thirteen, Red-Green Alliance eleven, Social Liberal Party ten, Denmark Democrats ten, Alternative five, and the Citizens' Party four.

83
00:11:22.378 --> 00:11:50.976
In percent of the vote: Social Democrats twenty-one point nine, SF eleven point six, Venstre ten point one, Liberal Alliance nine point four, Danish People's Party nine point one, Moderates seven point seven, Conservatives seven point six, Red-Green Alliance six point three, Social Liberal Party five point eight, Denmark Democrats five point eight, Alternative two point six, and Citizens' Party two point one.

84
00:11:51.526 --> 00:11:55.713
A cheat sheet for the questions about which party is oldest or newest.

85
00:11:55.663 --> 00:12:00.426
The oldest are Venstre and Højre, both organised around 1870.

86
00:12:00.426 --> 00:12:07.988
Højre was renamed the Conservative People's Party in 1915 to 16, so the Conservatives count as an old party.

87
00:12:07.988 --> 00:12:12.338
The Social Democrats became an independent party in 1878.

88
00:12:12.338 --> 00:12:36.450
The Social Liberal Party came in 1905, SF in 1959, the Red-Green Alliance in 1989, the Danish People's Party was first elected in 1998, Liberal Alliance in 2011, the Alternative in 2015, the Moderates and the Denmark Democrats in 2022, and the Citizens' Party in 2024.

89
00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:39.337
That completes the history chapter.

90
00:12:39.288 --> 00:12:42.087
Here is your final recap for Episode Six.

91
00:12:42.087 --> 00:12:48.438
The Berlin Wall fell in November 1989, and the Soviet Union dissolved in 1991.

92
00:12:48.438 --> 00:12:59.962
The Maastricht Treaty was rejected with fifty point seven percent in June 1992, and the Edinburgh Agreement was approved with fifty-six point seven percent in 1993.

93
00:12:59.962 --> 00:13:10.887
The four opt-outs were Union citizenship, the euro, defence, and justice and immigration, and two still apply: the euro and justice and immigration.

94
00:13:10.887 --> 00:13:14.262
The Danish People's Party entered in 1998.

95
00:13:14.262 --> 00:13:19.000
Anders Fogh Rasmussen was prime minister 2001 to 2009.

96
00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:21.950
The twenty-four-year rule applies to spouses.

97
00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:32.475
Afghanistan in 2002, Iraq in 2003, the Muhammad drawings in 2005, and the Copenhagen terror attack in 2015.

98
00:13:32.475 --> 00:13:35.600
The financial crisis hit in 2008.

99
00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:41.762
Helle Thorning-Schmidt was the first female prime minister, 2011 to 2015.

100
00:13:41.762 --> 00:13:45.862
Covid-19 reached Denmark in February 2020.

101
00:13:45.862 --> 00:13:52.862
Poul Nyrup Rasmussen of the Social Democrats was prime minister from 1993 to 2001.

102
00:13:52.862 --> 00:13:57.825
Anders Fogh Rasmussen became NATO Secretary General in 2009.

103
00:13:57.825 --> 00:14:02.087
The Red-Green Alliance was first elected in 1994.

104
00:14:02.087 --> 00:14:05.612
The 2015 attack also targeted the synagogue.

105
00:14:05.612 --> 00:14:08.575
Helle Thorning-Schmidt is a Social Democrat.

106
00:14:08.575 --> 00:14:15.737
Mette Frederiksen has been prime minister since 2019, and the latest election was in March 2026.

107
00:14:15.737 --> 00:14:18.900
Congratulations, you have finished Chapter One.

108
00:14:19.450 --> 00:14:22.050
Now it is time for your exam practice.

109
00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:26.913
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

110
00:14:26.913 --> 00:14:34.398
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

111
00:14:34.848 --> 00:14:36.720
Question 1.

112
00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:42.264
Which former Danish prime minister became Secretary General of NATO in 2009?

113
00:14:42.264 --> 00:14:45.528
Option A: Poul Nyrup Rasmussen.

114
00:14:45.528 --> 00:14:48.528
Option B: Anders Fogh Rasmussen.

115
00:14:48.528 --> 00:14:51.120
Option C: Helle Thorning-Schmidt.
[DA] Hvilken tidligere dansk statsminister blev i 2009 generalsekretær for NATO?  A: Poul Nyrup Rasmussen  |  B: Anders Fogh Rasmussen  |  C: Helle Thorning-Schmidt

116
00:14:57.670 --> 00:15:00.833
The answer is B: Anders Fogh Rasmussen.

117
00:15:00.783 --> 00:15:05.322
It appeared in the November 2021 and November 2025 exams.
[DA] ✔ B: Anders Fogh Rasmussen

118
00:15:05.872 --> 00:15:07.122
Question 2.

119
00:15:07.072 --> 00:15:09.960
Who was Denmark's first female prime minister?

120
00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:12.647
Option A: Margrethe Vestager.

121
00:15:12.647 --> 00:15:15.010
Option B: Helle Thorning-Schmidt.

122
00:15:15.010 --> 00:15:17.604
Option C: Mette Frederiksen.
[DA] Hvem var Danmarks første kvindelige statsminister?  A: Margrethe Vestager  |  B: Helle Thorning-Schmidt  |  C: Mette Frederiksen

123
00:15:24.154 --> 00:15:26.779
The answer is B: Helle Thorning-Schmidt.

124
00:15:26.729 --> 00:15:29.622
It appeared in the November 2022 exam.
[DA] ✔ B: Helle Thorning-Schmidt

125
00:15:30.172 --> 00:15:31.510
Question 3.

126
00:15:31.460 --> 00:15:38.010
